$DIR/main.p:21:2: w holds a view of r (r, line 19), which was moved into use (its parameter takes a Res by value) at line 20, so w can no longer be used here
    w looks at memory that r owns, without owning it: the view was made by r at
    line 19. A view of an owner held in a local variable may be used only while
    the variable keeps the value the view was made from. At line 20 r was moved
    into use (its parameter takes a Res by value), so the view may look at
    memory that was released, or at a value that now belongs to something else.
    Go keeps memory alive for as long as any pointer refers to it. Plutonium has
    no garbage collector: an owner releases its memory when it is dropped, so a
    view must not be used after the value it looks at has gone.
    Use w before line 20, or move r only after the last use of w
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3313" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:28:10: f was converted to Res at line 27, so it can no longer be used here
    owned[File] is an owner type: its values own what they hold and release it
    when they are dropped. Assigning an owner moves it instead of copying it, so
    that what it owns has exactly one owner. Since line 27, f holds nothing.
    Go copies such values freely and its garbage collector frees what they refer
    to when nothing refers to it any more. Plutonium has no garbage collector:
    an owner frees what it owns when it is dropped, so there must be exactly
    one.
    If this is the last use of f, move it before line 27, where f still holds
    its value.
    (see "Moves" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.5)
    (run "pluto explain E3301" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:35:10: f was moved to r at line 34, so it can no longer be used here
    owned[File] is an owner type: its values own what they hold and release it
    when they are dropped. Assigning an owner moves it instead of copying it, so
    that what it owns has exactly one owner. Since line 34, r owns it and f
    holds nothing.
    Go copies such values freely and its garbage collector frees what they refer
    to when nothing refers to it any more. Plutonium has no garbage collector:
    an owner frees what it owns when it is dropped, so there must be exactly
    one.
    If r only needs to look at the value, give it a pointer instead:
        r := &f
    If this is the last use of f, move it before line 34, where f still holds
    its value.
    (see "Moves" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.5)
    (run "pluto explain E3301" for a longer explanation)
